── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2550  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Ambiguous 'optional' qualifier on mseccfg CSR decouples its existence from the extensions it controls, enabling hardware to omit security configuration while still exposing controlled features.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:245-249  (header definitions)
      MSECCFG_PMM and MSECCFG_PMM_PMLEN_* macros are defined as unconditional aliases to ENVCFG_PMM values. They are currently dead code — no kernel C file references these macros (all PMM configuration uses ENVCFG via CSR_ENVCFG/S-mode access). However, the definitions carry no guard, no capability-probe infrastructure, and no comment warning that mseccfg is optional per the RISC-V spec. Any future code that reads/writes mseccfg through these macros without first checking its existence will fault on hardware that implements pointer-masking but omits the mseccfg CSR. The alias-to-ENVCFG pattern further subtly implies that mseccfg bit layout is always identical to ENVCFG, which is spec-true when mseccfg exists but not guaranteed across all implementations.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:429-430  (header definitions)
      CSR_MSECCFG (0x747) and CSR_MSECCFGH (0x757) are defined unconditionally alongside always-present CSRs like MCAUSE and MHARTID. No code references these constants — they are dead. However, the header provides zero infrastructure to distinguish conditional CSRs (mseccfg, which the spec treats as optional) from mandatory CSRs. There is no riscv_has_mseccfg() probe helper, no CONFIG_RISCV_MSECCFG gate, and no comment documenting the existence ambiguity. Any future SBI or M-mode proxy code that passes CSR_MSECCFG to firmware without a prior existence check risks a fault on compliant hardware that advertises PMM/Zkr but omits mseccfg — exactly the 'semantic gap' described in the security spec.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2550  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Ambiguous 'optional' qualifier on mseccfg CSR decouples its existence from the extensions it controls, enabling hardware to omit security configuration while still exposing controlled features.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:727-740  (header definitions)
      This is the canonical definition site for CSR_MSECCFG (0x747), CSR_MSECCFGH (0x757), and all bitfield macros (MML, MMWP, RLB, USEED, SSEED). The definitions are unconditional — no #ifdef guard, no has_mseccfg() helper macro, no existence-probe infrastructure exists anywhere in this header. This is fine as a constant-definition site, but the absence of a guard/probe helper means all downstream callers must enforce their own correctness, which creates the risk surface analysed in the other locations.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:139-139  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      csr_set(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_RLB) sets Rule-Lock Bypass before programming PMP entries. The access is gated indirectly: this function is only reachable when sbi_hart_has_extension(SMEPMP) is true (checked at sbi_hart_pmp.c:335 before registering epmp_protection). However, SMEPMP extension detection comes from FDT ISA-string parsing (riscv,isa-extensions or riscv,isa), not from a CSR-existence trap-probe. The RISC-V spec describes mseccfg as 'optional', creating ambiguity: a hardware implementation could advertise Smepmp in its ISA string (because PMP encoding changes exist) but omit the mseccfg CSR itself. If mseccfg is absent, this csr_set will trap in M-mode with no recovery path. The implicit invariant 'SMEPMP extension → mseccfg CSR exists' is not enforced in code.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:185-185  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      csr_set(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_MML) enables Machine Mode Lockdown after programming M-only PMP entries. Same gating assumption as line 139: reachable only when SMEPMP extension is detected via FDT, but no trap-probe verifies mseccfg CSR actually exists. If the CSR is absent, this write traps in M-mode. Note that line 139 would trap first, so line 185 is never reached in the 'missing mseccfg' scenario, but it carries the identical assumption.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:176-180  mstatus_init
      csr_set(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_SSEED) and csr_clear(CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_USEED) configure S-mode access to the seed CSR under Zkr. The access is gated on two conditions: (1) priv_version >= 1.12 (line 137), and (2) sbi_hart_has_extension(ZKR) (line 177). The risk here is higher than the SMEPMP case: ZKR does NOT architecturally guarantee mseccfg existence in the same way SMEPMP does. mseccfg is defined as part of the SMEPMP extension, while ZKR merely consumes two bits within it. A hardware implementation that provides the seed CSR (ZKR) but omits SMEPMP could legally omit mseccfg, leaving SSEED/USEED unconfigurable. In that scenario, these csr_set/csr_clear operations would trap. The code implicitly assumes ZKR → mseccfg exists, which is the exact semantic gap the security spec warns about.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:698-706  hart_detect_features
      csr_read(CSR_MSECCFG) reads mseccfg.MML at boot to assert the previous boot stage left MML=0. Gated on sbi_hart_has_extension(SMEPMP) only. This function runs during cold boot before any trap handlers are installed; if SMEPMP is advertised in the FDT ISA string but mseccfg is absent in hardware, this csr_read traps fatally. The code relies on the implicit invariant 'SMEPMP extension flag → mseccfg CSR physically present', which is not verified via a trap-probe. Note that hart_detect_features uses csr_read_allowed/csr_write_allowed (with trap capture) for other CSRs (e.g., PMPADDR0 detection at line 588-593, mstateen at 653-654) but uses a bare csr_read for mseccfg — inconsistent probing discipline.

